window.WATER_HARVESTING = {"schema_version":1,"source_page":"orchard_water_harvesting_plan.html","climate_source":{"precipitation":"PRISM 1991-2020 normals, 800 m, 43.50 N / -115.57 E","et0":"ERA5 via Open-Meteo, FAO-56, 1995-2024"},"months":[{"month":1,"precipitation_in":3.12,"et0_in":0.83},{"month":2,"precipitation_in":2.22,"et0_in":1.22},{"month":3,"precipitation_in":2.73,"et0_in":2.28},{"month":4,"precipitation_in":2.11,"et0_in":3.38},{"month":5,"precipitation_in":1.99,"et0_in":4.97},{"month":6,"precipitation_in":1.02,"et0_in":6.25},{"month":7,"precipitation_in":0.31,"et0_in":7.86},{"month":8,"precipitation_in":0.27,"et0_in":6.88},{"month":9,"precipitation_in":0.64,"et0_in":4.68},{"month":10,"precipitation_in":1.5,"et0_in":2.7},{"month":11,"precipitation_in":2.4,"et0_in":1.22},{"month":12,"precipitation_in":3.62,"et0_in":0.72}],"assumptions":{"gallons_per_sqft_in":0.623,"mature_canopy_factor":0.8,"root_depth_ft":[3,4]},"soil":{"name":"Simonton loam, 2 to 12 percent slopes","unit":"149","group":"C","awc60":9.3,"precip":[12,16],"frostFree":[60,100],"ph":6.2,"layers":[{"hz":"A","top":0,"bottom":4,"texture":"Loam","clay":15,"ksat":[0.57,1.28,2],"awc":0.17,"color":"#C9A77F","note":"The sponge layer: most of the organic matter (about 1.5%), and it takes water fast. Keep it in basin floors and keep it covered."},{"hz":"Bt","top":4,"bottom":42,"texture":"Clay loam","clay":28,"ksat":[0.2,0.4,0.6],"awc":0.18,"color":"#B5835E","note":"The bottleneck: it sets how fast basins drain, and it holds most of the root zone's water."},{"hz":"C","top":42,"bottom":60,"texture":"Sandy loam","clay":7.5,"ksat":[2,4,6],"awc":0.1,"color":"#DCC69F","note":"Drains freely: no hardpan or water table, and about 10 ft of soil to basalt lava (owner, October 28, 2026). The Simonton soil is the cover. The well is in the lava under it."}]},"fruit_guidance":{"Apple":["Moderate","Full crescent; spillway 3\u20134 in above the floor","Yes: first claim","The highest, best-drained trees, first in line for the homestead water. The 13 reserved R1 spots get basins when the grafted apples go in."],"Pear":["Best of your fruit trees (UC IPM)","Full crescent; spillway 3\u20134 in above the floor","Yes: can take the most","A natural catch for overflow coming down from the apples."],"Cherry":["Low: can't take saturated soil (UC IPM)","Small; spillway 3 in or less; tree on a low mound","No: let run-on pass by","Bacterial canker is flagged on several; UC IPM says it is worse in low spots and after spring freezes."],"Peach":["Low: can't take saturated soil (UC IPM)","Small; spillway 3 in or less; tree on a low mound","No","Canker flagged on most; early bloom."],"Apricot":["Low (stone fruit)","Smallest; if the perc test is slow, add a low uphill berm that steers run-on around the tree","No","The lowest planted trees and the earliest bloom; canker flagged on the apricots."],"Plum":["Low to moderate","Small; spillway 3 in or less","Little","No canker flags in the inventory."]},"techniques":[{"id":"water-zone-1","zone":"1. Homestead (the high point)","work":"Start here. Gutter the red shed, the two containers, the pump house, and any future house into tanks for early-summer watering, or straight into the spreader below. Let winter snow slide off roofs into basins, not onto paths. Send the gravel pad's runoff north in a gently sloped diversion swale to the spreader.","specifications":"Each 1,000 sq ft of roof sheds about 620 gal per inch, about 4,900 gal from March through June. A tank at the homestead sits above the orchard rows and gives about 0.43 psi per foot of drop: enough for low-pressure gravity drip, not for your 15 psi drip tape without a pump. Use the calculator on the budget page for your own areas.","reference":"Vol. 1 Ch. 3 (Cistern Systems); Vol. 2 Ch. 8 (Hardscape Runoff), Ch. 9 (Diversion Swales)","timing":"Spring\u2013summer 2027"},{"id":"water-zone-2","zone":"2. Spreader above R1","work":"Catch the homestead water, and any sheet flow from upslope, in short, staggered on-contour berm 'n basins across the top of the block, spilling into the R1\u2013R2 basins. The apples there are the best-drained trees and the best able to use it. Contours here cross the rows at a slant (17\u201356\u00b0), so a single long contour line would cut through rows: keep each segment short and level.","specifications":"Segments 30\u201360 ft long, 3\u20134 in of water depth, level crests, spillways at the ends lined with sod (no rock needed)","reference":"Vol. 2 Ch. 3 (Berm 'n Basins), Ch. 9","timing":"With the first apples"},{"id":"water-zone-3","zone":"3. Every orchard tree","work":"A crescent berm 'n basin on the downhill side (northwest to north-northwest here), with its spillway aimed at the next tree downhill, usually the northwest diagonal. Plant on the basin's upper edge, never in its bottom. Put the drip emitters inside the basin.","specifications":"Start 3\u20134 ft from the trunk; widen toward the drip line as the tree grows. Tree on a low mound at the upper edge; level floor dug no deeper than about 2 in, so it stays above the slower clay-loam subsoil. Spillway 3\u20134 in above the floor (3 in or less for stone fruit), lined with sod; berm crest level and 2\u20133 in above it; low and rounded so you can mow over it.","reference":"Vol. 2 Ch. 3 (Berm 'n Basins)","timing":"At planting (now)"},{"id":"water-zone-4","zone":"4. Orchard floor","work":"Living sponge: wood chips in the basins (chip the windbreak and orchard prunings), perennial grass-and-clover cover in the alleys, clippings mowed or blown onto the tree rows. Mulched soil freezes less deeply, so snowmelt can soak in instead of sheeting off frozen ground. Stay off soft rows during snowmelt; ruts become ditches running west.","specifications":"Chips 2\u20133 in deep in basins (about 2 in for stone fruit), surface at least 1 in below the spillway, kept 6\u201312 in back from trunks; 3\u20134 in elsewhere. Hardware-cloth trunk guards: mulch plus snow cover hides voles.","reference":"Vol. 2 Ch. 7 (Mulching)","timing":"At planting; top up yearly"},{"id":"water-zone-5","zone":"5. Driveway (east side)","work":"It runs downhill along the orchard, and the land tilts west, so its runoff, plus some from the higher neighbor's side, heads into the headland. Catch it in a chain of shallow, mowable basins along the driveway's west edge, stepped down the slope by low check berms, each spilling west to the east-end (C13) trees. If the drive is gravel, broad rolling dips across it kick water into the basins. Push salt-free plowed snow into the basins, not onto young plants.","specifications":"Basins about 6 ft wide with 3\u20134 in of water depth; the drive falls about 3% along the block, so a check berm about every 12\u201315 ft keeps each cell level. Each 100 ft of the 22 ft drive sheds up to about 1,370 gal per inch of rain. Never route salted snow or oily runoff to plants.","reference":"Vol. 2 Ch. 8; Ch. 5 (Infiltration Trenches)","timing":"Spring\u2013summer 2027"},{"id":"water-zone-6","zone":"6. Elderberry hedge","work":"It sits upslope of most of the driveway's runoff, so it lives on its own rain and snow. If the drive is crowned and its east half drains east, small crescents on each bush's downhill side catch that water.","specifications":"Crescents 2\u20133 ft out, 3\u20134 in of water depth","reference":"Vol. 2 Ch. 3","timing":"As the cuttings root"},{"id":"water-zone-7","zone":"7. West windbreak (snow fence)","work":"Snow-drift harvesting: west winds will drop drifts just east of the hazelnut row, over the tractor alley and the C1 berry guilds. Let them melt in place. Brush heavy snow off young limbs, and expect the alley to stay blocked until the drift melts.","specifications":"Drifts grow with the windbreak. A dense, bushy bottom piles snow close; a more open row spreads it farther and thinner.","reference":"Vol. 1 App. 8; Vol. 2 Ch. 3 (\"Harvesting Windswept Snow\")","timing":"Every winter"},{"id":"water-zone-8","zone":"8. Tractor alley","work":"Low, broad, drivable diagonal humps (water bars) so wheel ruts can't turn into a gully running north. Each one turns water west into the hazelnut row.","specifications":"Every 75\u2013100 ft; 4\u20136 in high; angled about 30\u00b0 across the alley","reference":"Vol. 2 Ch. 9 (Diversion Swales)","timing":"When ruts appear"},{"id":"water-zone-9","zone":"9. Walking lane","work":"Make the mulberry\u2013hazelnut lane a sunken wood-chip path between the two planted rows, so it soaks up overflow for both. Low cross-berms keep water from running north along it.","specifications":"Cross-berms 4\u20136 in high every 20\u201325 ft (the west side falls about 2%)","reference":"Vol. 1 Ch. 4, pattern 6 (Raised Paths, Sunken Basins)","timing":"When the lane is laid out"},{"id":"water-zone-10","zone":"10. Northwest corner (by the road)","work":"The final overflow \"sponge\": a broad, shallow infiltration basin planted with wet-tolerant, later-blooming shrubs such as elderberry (from your hardwood cuttings) and aronia. Keep it inside your line and back from the road so overflow never cuts the road edge, and keep it in the corner rather than a solid hedge across the north end, so cold air can still drain away.","specifications":"6 in deep or less and wide; size it after watching one snowmelt; give it a spillway too.","reference":"Vol. 2 Ch. 2 (Infiltration Basins)","timing":"After a season of watching"}],"calendar":[{"season":"Oct\u2013Nov (now)","conditions":"Planting; first snow (November averages 2.4 in of water)","work":"Shape a crescent as each tree goes in; add chips and trunk guards; flag the fall line at a few trees; run a perc test in three spots before the ground freezes. If fall stays dry, give one deep drip watering before freeze-up. Set up a rain gauge; CoCoRaHS volunteers report daily, snow included."},{"season":"Dec\u2013Feb","conditions":"Snowiest months (about 9 in of water)","work":"Push salt-free plowed snow into the driveway-edge basins, never onto young plants; note where drifts form; brush heavy snow off young limbs; let roof snow slide into basins."},{"season":"Mar\u2013Apr","conditions":"Snowmelt plus spring rain (about 4.8 in): the main harvest","work":"Walk the block during melt and after rain-on-snow; log where water runs and ponds; check that basins empty within about a day; repair breached berms; stay off soft rows; open the gutters to the tanks."},{"season":"May\u2013Jun","conditions":"Last dependable rain (about 3 in); bloom and leaf-out","work":"Start the drip only when the soil 6\u201312 in down is drying (check with a trowel or probe); widen crescents; top up chips; mow and blow the alleys onto the rows."},{"season":"Jul\u2013Sep","conditions":"Nearly dry (about 1.2 in in all); demand peaks (ET\u2080 about 7.9 in in July)","work":"Drip runs inside the basins, topped up from the roof tanks while they last; after any thunderstorm, check for erosion and where the runoff went."},{"season":"Late Sep\u2013Oct","conditions":"Reassess (principle 8)","work":"Widen basins toward the drip line, fix trouble spots, drain tanks and hoses before hard freezes, and choose the next project (spreader, driveway basins, lane, corner basin)."}],"catchments":[{"id":"catchment-1","name":"Red shed roof","area_sqft":null,"runoff_factor":0.9,"notes":"Area is not measured; map roof footprints are not assumed to include eaves."},{"id":"catchment-2","name":"Container roofs (two)","area_sqft":null,"runoff_factor":0.9,"notes":"Area is not measured; map roof footprints are not assumed to include eaves."},{"id":"catchment-3","name":"Pump house roof","area_sqft":null,"runoff_factor":0.9,"notes":"Area is not measured; map roof footprints are not assumed to include eaves."},{"id":"catchment-4","name":"House roof (future)","area_sqft":null,"runoff_factor":0.9,"notes":"Area is not measured; map roof footprints are not assumed to include eaves."},{"id":"catchment-5","name":"Gravel house pad","area_sqft":null,"runoff_factor":0.5,"notes":"Area is not measured; map roof footprints are not assumed to include eaves."},{"id":"catchment-6","name":"Other","area_sqft":null,"runoff_factor":0.5,"notes":"Area is not measured; map roof footprints are not assumed to include eaves."}],"observations":[]};
